Subject: Chaitin, Complexity, and the Thermodynamics of Information

Ah, the discussion will soon get more interesting! Derek Zahn writes:

>So I went to the library to check this out, and Lo!, right next to it,
>a book that looked even *better*!
>
>Chaitin, Gregory J.
>Information, Randomness, and Incompleteness
>World Scientific, Singapore, 1987.
>ISBN 9971-50-479-0
>
>This book is a collection of 21 of Chaitin's papers.  TOC:
.....
>Hardy fare for information-hungry extropian!
>
>derek
>say, this will replace Korzybski on the night-table... I got tired of
>him NEVER GETTING TO THE POINT.  Is there a better (briefer, more
>readable) work on the topic than _Science and Sanity_?
>
>oh, and what's a "logical tile"?

I'm glad the profundities of Chaitin have replaced the obscurities of the
Count ("Count Zero," I call him) on Derek's reading list.

Yes, Chaitin's little book (the paperback *still* costs $40, which I why I
refuse on principle to own it when I can Xerox the best articles so much
more economically!) contains several good articles, including his
"Scientific American" aritcles on randomness. As I recall, though, the 1987
collection does *not* contain his crowning conclusion, made in his new
results book published the same year (which I *did* pay $40 for...at least
it was a nice hardback.)

Chaitin also has a new (1992) book out, summarizing his points, but I've
barely looked at it so far.

Ironically, I just checked out Count Zero's "Science and Sanity" as some
final reading for my 250K-plus (gulp!) report on the Paganism business
(obviously my net has extended into epistemology, the philosophy of
science, maps of reality, operational truth, etc.), I found K. as
inscrutable last night as I did in 1971. So much for the pristine clarity
of general semantics! (Even worse were some newsletters and yearbooks put
out by the Inst. for General Semantics, which UCSC has a complete
collection of...mostly crankish ramblings and pictures of members,)

"Logical tiles" are the locally-logical tilings of a plane, for example,
where some constraint--such as edges matching shapes or colors--must be
satisfied. Think of these as "dominoes" which are to be snaked across a
table-top while meeting some logical constraint (like the numbers on the
ends matching). 

- Can a domino snake be extended from Point A to Point B? (with the tiles
or dominoes in a grid, so no funny bending is allowed)

- Can a given finite region be tiled with some given set of tiles?

These problems may sound mundane, but they have been shown to be isomorphic
(the same structure as) logic in general...tilings are theorems (with the
specific shapes or edge constraints of the tiles being the axioms or
previously proved theorems of the system...the "legal moves," as it were). 

Problems of whether a point can be reached by a domino snake are related to
problems of undecidability. (To make this clearer, a mere 10 x 10 grid has
more possible snakes than computers can, or ever will, handle. If points A
and B are, say separated by 10 grid spaces in the X direction and 10 spaces
in the Y direction, it is intractable to consider all snakes which lie
within this 10 x 10 array. A 100 x 100 grid is so vastly more intractable
as to boggle any mind, even  a Jupiter-sized one! And if snakes are allowed
go out to Yonkers, down to Atlanta, over to Chicago, etc., the answer is
utterly beyond comprehension. And yet the logical constraint on snakes
means these are "true theorems" in this system. (The snakes are free to go
as far in any direction as one wishes...clearly the problems blows up very
quickly!)

The tiling problem (Huang tiles, for math folks, and mentioned in Grunbaum
and Sheperd's "Tilings" book) has deep resonances with the "landscape of
reality" model/metaphor I have used several times. 

To bring this home for Extropians, imagine that "out there" in knowledge
space (I've mentioned k-space before, but can't go into right now) is a
true nanotechnology-based "general assembler." How do we get there?

We're busy exploring k-space, extending truths in certain areas, building
support structures...basically, we humans are "colonizing" this landscape,
just as settlers moved into uncharted territory and built tools, support
lines, etc. In the tiling metaphor (which is more than a metaphor), the
logical constraints correspond to the requirement that local laws of
mathematics, physics, etc., be observed. (You can't build arbitrarily.)

Now right now we don't know which of the "domino snakes" will take us from
where we are to the place in k-space that's the "general assembler."
Knowing that such a gadget is possible is *not* enough to know how to get
there.

Now of course once we're there, the path will look "retroactively obvious,"
just as the path to the microprocessor look obvious to us today (but is
sure as hell didn't to the many bright people in 1970 looking ahead!). The
"future is nearly opaque, past is nearly transparent" model is closely
connected to the concepts of NP-completeness (a solution is hard to find in
the first place, but once a solution is found or presented or even guessed,
it is trivially easy to verify...a domino snake between two points may be
incredibly hard to find, but can be easily verified once found).

Nothing here means nanotech is hopeless. Far from it. But my point is that
knowing an assembler probably "exists" is not enough. (By the way, here's
where Platonism shows off its strength...don't most of us believe that
Drexler's vague ideas about a real general assembler actually "exist" in
some kind of Hilbert space of knowledge?) Colonizing the space in the
direction means stable economic returns, fertile soil, etc. The analogy
with the microprocessor is nearly perfect. George Dumbar (or Dumbart?)
forecast in the early 1950s that someday a component would be built with a
million or more elements and it would act as a computer. But he was
ignored, rightly so (in my opinion), because such a forecast said almost
nothing about the hundreds of intermediate steps, the thousands of workers,
the products, the failures and successes, and so on. We have built a domino
snake--a stable pathway in k-space--between Dumbar's vision of 40 years ago
and the million transistor processors of today. We figured out how to tile
this part of space.

(Obviously I included economic, social, and legal constraints as part of
the various "legal moves" of the system.)

To my mind, understanding the nature of this "knowledge space," especially
for product development and economic exploitation, is of great Extropian
relevance. Nick Szabo agrees with me, and has used this model in some of
his own work.

Thus, my model of reality, sketched above (sorry for the succinctness), is
that knowledge spreads in a process of colonization, or digging out a mine
in k-space, or tiling with many of these locally-consistent tile snakes.

-Tim May, Explorer and Colonizer of Knowledge Space

