Date: Mon, 17 May 93 21:50:32 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: FTL: Traversable Wormholes

I've recently posted a 41K file on wormholes to the exi-essay list.  I'm
looking for feedback about its style and content before submitting it
for publication in extropy#11.  In particular I'm interested in any bits
that seem unnecessarily obtuse (or incorrect).  It mostly recapitulates
the material covered in the recent FTL thread, but I've emphasized the
return to a conception of Newtonian time as absolute and universal a bit
more than previously.

Here is the summary:

     Since 1985 there has been much theoretical progress on
     traversable wormholes.  At first they were thought to enable
     time travel, so not taken seriously.  More recent work rules
     out time travel and associated paradoxes, but still permits
     faster-than-light travel.  This article explores some of the
     implications traversable wormholes have on the expansion of
     civilisations through the universe.  In particular it is found
     each civilisation imposes a local region of simultaneity, or
     empire-time, which differs from the more natural, co-moving
     timeframe which astrophysicists.  Distant regions of the
     universe and alien civilisations can be reached in short
     periods of empire-time, whereupon their respective empire-time
     zones fuse together.  Shortly after first contact is made all
     expanding civilisations connect together to form a universal
     time.  Finally some of the information processing limitations
     of euclidean space are contrasted with wormhole connected non-
     euclidean space.

