Date: Mon, 17 May 1993 18:57:01 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: PHIL/MATH: Stronger results than Godel's

I've been thinking about the excerpt Tim May posted on Chaitin's omega theorem.
My initial reaction was that I was disappointed because it doesn't actually
seem to me to be a significantly stronger result than Godel's Incompleteness
theorem itself.  To say that it shows "arithmetic is random" certainly seems
to overstate the case.

What *would* constitute a stronger result would be a proof that the set of
decidable theorems, within the set of all well-formed propositions, is in some
sense, a sparse one.  The equivalence Chaitin trades on suggests a way to
approach this question, because it implies a topology on the set of all
theorems within which one can characterize at least one infinite point set
of undecideables.

I'm also bugged more strongly than ever by the suspicion that the Intuitionists
had something right --- Chaitin's theorem has the same artificial-seeming twist-
an-actual-infinity-back-on-itself character found in all the nastiest
indefiniteness results.

Kryzs, would you care to expatiate on how to rescue mathematics from
actual infinities?  Your reference to Jan M<something-Polish> made it sound
like there's been more constructive work on this than I'm aware of.
-- 
							>>eric>>

