Like many other words, the same word "probability" is used to refer to
several distinct concepts.  The "frequency of occurance in a large
set" meaning, which you seem to prefer, is indeed difficult to apply
to many situations.  The "degree of belief" concept, however, applies
more broadly, though it is of course relative to an actor holding
beliefs.

>Would their be a difference between a market which was
>absolutely positive that Clinton had a 50% chance of winning, versus one
>which was uncertain and considered Clinton's chances to be in the 10-80%
>range I mentioned above?  

Sure.  The first market would be very thick; if you tried to move the
price by buying on one side, folks would rush in to move it back to
50%.  In the second case, traders might think you had info they
didn't, and let you move the price more freely.

>I meant to describe a situation where there was no information about what
>was likely to happen, hence I need to postulate that the people don't
>know me.  Perhaps we should consider the problem to be posed by an alien
>being about whose psychology we know nothing.

I don't think the concept of "no information" makes sense.  You always
know/believe something.

Nick Szabo comments:
>The uncertainty market players have about the 50% is reflected in the 
>price volatility, or the standard deviation in the price per unit time,
>assuming the market players are rational.   

Volatility reflects the rate at which information comes out.
Information rate is in principle independent of how "sure" the market
is about that 50% now.

