Here the brevity of English is the problem, as I have no idea what you mean
by "overstate the case." Maybe  in e-prime? What Chaitin showed, as the
Casti explanation hopefully conveyed, is that randomness is apparently
wired in a deeper level than previously suspected.

In another post, I will have more on this.

>
>What *would* constitute a stronger result would be a proof that the set of
>decidable theorems, within the set of all well-formed propositions, is in some
>sense, a sparse one.  The equivalence Chaitin trades on suggests a way to
>approach this question, because it implies a topology on the set of all
>theorems within which one can characterize at least one infinite point set
>of undecideables.

But this is just what Chaitin's result shows! 

Heinz Pagels, in "The Dreams of Reason," 1988, pp. 302-303, writes:

"Michael Rabin, an Israeli mathematician, once characterized the realm of
mathematics with the following wonderful image. Imagine the set of all true
propositions about mathematics as occupying a vast space--the space of all
true theorems. Some of these theorems have been proven,  a rather small set
of all the true theorems, and these we imagine as a thin illuminated line,
a bright string with a few branches transversing this dark space. The dark
part of the vast space consists of all true theorems that we have not
proven and that probably never will be proven. The reason we cannot prove
these true theorems is that the proofs require such a long time to
calculate that even the largest supercomputer grinding away for the
lifetime of the universe cannot accomplish the proof. They require such a
large but finite computing time that we will never know if they are true.
Other "true" theorems might require an infinite computing time -- these are
the theorems that are undecidable within a specific axiom system. The
bright line shows us the true and provable theorems, the ones that don't
require so much time, the ones that ingenious mathematicians can find using
human skills."

This dark space of true theorems is characterized by Chaitin's uncomputable
number Omega. If Omega could be computed, then one could just look at the
expansion of it to some number of decimal places and see if some particular
theorem was true or not!

(My tiling metaphor from my last posting is of course very similar....we
have sent out "snakes" to some tiny fraction of true theorems...the "bright
lines" in Rabin's metaphor. By the way, Rabin is a famous cryptologist.
Full circle.)

>
>I'm also bugged more strongly than ever by the suspicion that the Intuitionists
>had something right --- Chaitin's theorem has the same artificial-seeming
>twist-
>an-actual-infinity-back-on-itself character found in all the nastiest
>indefiniteness results.

Well, I wouldn't count on "intuition" to decide whether the Intuitionists
were right! Also, most of the Chaitin-Kolmogoroff-Turing-Bennett results
have more to do with real programs, real objects, and real issues of
compressibility, randomness, etc., than with infinities. Life, complexity,
evolution, and logica depth are all deeply interrelated. Many now view
Shannon's information theory (which has always been focused on
communication channels) as a subset of a more general approach to
complexity, that of algorithmic complexity theory.

-Tim May




