Robin Hanson writes:

>Timothy C. May scans John Casti's saying:
>> And there are an infinite number of such undecidable
>>digits, each corresponding to the following simple, definite arithmetical
>>fact: For that value of k in Chaitin's equation X = O, the equation has
>>either a finite or an infinite number of solutions. But as far as human
>>reasoning goes, which of the two possibilities is actually the case may as
>>well be decided by flipping a coin; it is completely and forever
>>undecidable, hence effectively random.
>
>An infinity of undecideable facts is not at all the same as saying
>"arithmetic is random".  I agree that this summary is misleading.
>

Robin's excerpt is itself somewhat misleading, as this brief paragraph
above is not the whole exposition of Chaitin's work (one reason I included
several pages). And even Casti is not saying simply that an infinity of
undecideable facts (which Godel of course knew about) is saying "arithmetic
is random."

Paul Davies, in "The Mind of God: The Scientific Basis for a Rational
World," 1992, covers some of the same ground as Casti (and Pagels, and
others, all deriving from Chaitin's work). He writes, beginning on p. 132:

"Armed with this algorithmic definition, Chaitin has been able to
demonstrate that randomness pervades all mathematics, including
arithmetic.....

[a result about Chaitin's "monster equation" is given]

"The implications of this result are startling....Once randomness has
entered mathematics it infests it through and through....There is
randomness and hence uncertainty in mathematics, just as in
physics...Chaitin believes that mathematics will have to be treated more
like the natural sciences, in which results depend on mixture of logic and
empirical discovery. One might even foresee universities with departments
of experimental mathematics."

I would urge all interested folks to first read the semi-popular accounts,
in Casti, in Pagels, in Davies, and in a few other places, and then perhaps
read bits and pieces of Chaitin's work. Our friend Charles Bennett, of
reversible computation fame, has also written extensively on this topic
(especially as it relates to "logical depth," a topic I've posted on
before).

-Tim May


