>>[...] about the excerpt Tim May posted on Chaitin's omega theorem.
>>[...] To say that it shows "arithmetic is random" certainly seems
>>to overstate the case.
> 
> Here the brevity of English is the problem, as I have no idea what you mean
> by "overstate the case." Maybe  in e-prime? What Chaitin showed, as the
> Casti explanation hopefully conveyed, is that randomness is apparently
> wired in a deeper level than previously suspected.

I meant that it seems to me to be over-interpreting the omega theorem to claim
that "arithmetic is random".  All right, so the digits of omega are random and
the cardinality of the "omega set" (the set of theorems mapped to the digits
of omega) is aleph-null.  That doesn't imply anything much about the
*complement* of the omega set --- not even anything about its cardinality!

(To review: the kth digit of omega, according to your explanation,
was to be 1 if the set of solutions of X(k) is finite, 0 if infinite, for
X(k) a particular hairy Diophantine form of kth degree.  Have I got that
right?)

The key point here is that the omega set doesn't cover the space of all
WFFs.  It doesn't even come close.
 
>>What *would* constitute a stronger result would be a proof that the set of
>>decidable theorems, within the set of all well-formed propositions, is in some
>>sense, a sparse one.  The equivalence Chaitin trades on suggests a way to
>>approach this question, because it implies a topology on the set of all
>>theorems within which one can characterize at least one infinite point set
>>of undecideables.
> 
> But this is just what Chaitin's result shows! 

Maybe, but if so neither your explanation nor Costi's has made that clear.
The demonstration that there is a countably infinite set of undecideables
does *not* suffice to prove this more general result.  Omega-bar, the complement
set of theorems *not shown to be undecideable*, is of equal cardinality to
omega's.
 
> "Michael Rabin, an Israeli mathematician, once characterized the realm of
> mathematics with the following wonderful image. Imagine the set of all true
> propositions about mathematics as occupying a vast space--the space of all
> true theorems. Some of these theorems have been proven,  a rather small set
> of all the true theorems, and these we imagine as a thin illuminated line,
> a bright string with a few branches transversing this dark space."

Fine.  This suggests that the set of theorems decidable in finite time is
finite and sparse in the set of all WFFs.  But that's trivially obvious, and not
at all the same as demonstrating that the set of WFFs decidable *in principle*
(in your terms, if one had infinite time) is sparse (which would be the
stronger result I was looking for).

> This dark space of true theorems is characterized by Chaitin's uncomputable
> number Omega. If Omega could be computed, then one could just look at the
> expansion of it to some number of decimal places and see if some particular
> theorem was true or not!

This might be true, *if the omega set exhausted the space of all theorems*.
Unless I have misunderstood Costi's explanation, it does not.

Now, let's suppose that the omega set *did* exhaust all theorems.  Then
the sparseness of the set of decidable WFFs would be proved.  However, the
claim that "arithmetic is random" would remain a rather meaningless bit of
rhetorical excess.

I'll argue that point if you convince me that my interpretation of the omega
theorem is broken.  In the mean time,...um, there's now a naked woman licking
my ear, which means I have more important things to attend to. :-)
